    <h1>Prompt framing and latent control states in language models</h1>
    <p class="!text-lg">{projects.latentControl.question}</p>
    <p>{projects.latentControl.summary}</p>
    <h2>Research question</h2>
    <p>Different prompt framings can change a model’s answer to the same dilemma.
      The project asks which changes in internal representations contribute to that behavioural
      difference, and how to distinguish them from effects of answer labels, display order,
      and the model’s understanding of the stated outcomes.</p>
    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <p>Controlled dilemma comparisons establish the behavioural contrast. Residual-stream
      patching then tests how replacing a state at a particular layer and token position
      changes the model’s choice. Low-rank analysis asks how much of that effect can be
      carried by a restricted subspace, with held-out items and random-subspace controls.</p>
    <p>The subspace is fitted without the held-out item, but its low-rank patch still uses
      that item’s donor difference. This evaluates the representation of an observed
      contrast; it does not by itself establish a fixed steering intervention for new inputs.</p>
    <h2>My contribution</h2>
    <p>I designed controlled dilemma comparisons and developed the experimental analysis
      around behavioural contrasts, activation interventions, and checks of competing explanations.
      The work runs over a year as a research project at UTN (2026–27), supervised by
      Michael Roth (UTN) and Simon Ostermann (DFKI) in biweekly meetings; a manuscript is in preparation with both.</p>
    <h2>Interpretation</h2>
    <p>Changing an activation and observing a changed answer is one part of the evidence.
      Answer-format effects and inconsistent outcome comprehension constrain how the
      result can be interpreted. The analysis keeps those diagnostics alongside the
      intervention results.</p>
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>The manuscript is in preparation and has not been submitted. Behavioural experiments
      and mechanistic analyses are being brought together into the paper.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
